 To apply a Gaussian filter before the Sobel edge detection in MATLAB, you need to add an extra step to your existing code. Here's how you can modify the provided code to apply a Gaussian filter followed by Sobel edge detection:

```MATLAB
% Read input image and convert to grayscale
input_image = imread('image.jpg');
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);

% Define Gaussian filter
gaussian_filter = fspecial('gaussian', [3,3], 2); % Standard deviation 2

% Apply Gaussian filter
filtered_image = imfilter(double(gray_image), gaussian_filter, 'same');

% Define Sobel operator in x and y directions
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];

% Apply Sobel operator in x and y directions
edge_image_x = conv2(filtered_image, sobel_x, 'same');
edge_image_y = conv2(filtered_image, sobel_y, 'same');

% Combine edges in both directions
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);

% Display original image, filtered image, and edge image
subplot(1,3,1), imshow(input_image); title('Original Image');
subplot(1,3,2), imshow(filtered_image); title('Filtered Image');
subplot(1,3,3), imshow(edge_image); title('Edge Image');
```

This code reads the input image, converts it to grayscale, applies a Gaussian filter using the `fspecial` function, and then performs Sobel edge detection on the filtered image. The resulting edge image is displayed alongside the original and filtered images for comparison.

