 To prevent the planets from being generated too close to each other, you can modify the `generateRandomPlanets` function by implementing a more efficient collision detection algorithm. One common method for this is using bounding spheres or bounding boxes for each planet. Here's an updated version of your function:

```typescript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
 const planets = [];
 let attempts = 0;

 while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
   attempts++;

   const planet = {
     position: [
       randomPosition(-30, 30),
       randomPosition(-30, 30),
       randomPosition(-30, 30),
     ],
     size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
     color: randomColor(),
     ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
     ringColor: randomColor(),
     ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
     bounds: new THREE.Box3().setFromObject(new THREE.SphereGeometry(planet.size / 2, 32, 32)), // create a bounding box for the planet
   };

   // Check if planet intersects with any existing planets
   const intersections = [];
   for (let i = 0; i < planets.length; i++) {
     const otherPlanet = planets[i];
     const intersection = new THREE.Intersection();

     planet.bounds.intersectsBox(otherPlanet.bounds, intersection);

     if (intersection.intersect) {
       intersections.push(true);
     } else {
       intersections.push(false);
     }
   }

   if (!intersections.includes(false)) {
     continue; // Skip this planet and try generating another one
   }

   planets.push(planet);
   break;
 }

 return planets;
}
```

